-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Wills Act 1997: the family's and beneficiaries' questions`

§§ `Who I am to the deceased`

@export Was I the deceased's domestic partner for the Wills Act 1997 (Vic) (s 3)? Yes if you were in a registered relationship with them at their death, or, not being married to them, were living with them as a couple on a genuine domestic basis at their death (of any gender).
GIVEN `registered` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were in a registered relationship (under the Relationships Act 2008 or a corresponding law) with the deceased at the date of death
      `married` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were married to the deceased at the date of death
      `living as a couple` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you were living with the deceased as a couple on a genuine domestic basis at the date of death, taking all the circumstances of the relationship into account
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Was I the deceased's domestic partner` MEANS
    `s 3 -- the domestic partner of the deceased` `registered` `married` `living as a couple`

@export Am I entitled to inspect and copy the deceased's will (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 50)? Whoever holds a will, a revoked will or a purported will of a deceased person must let the listed people inspect and copy it, at their own expense.
GIVEN `who` IS A `Who I am to the deceased` @desc Who you are: a person named or referred to in the will; a beneficiary named in an earlier will; the deceased's spouse at death; their domestic partner; their parent, guardian or child; a person who would share if there were no will; a parent or guardian of a minor named in the will or entitled without a will; a creditor or claimant against the estate with evidence of the claim; one without evidence; or someone else
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can I see the will` MEANS
    `s 50 -- I am entitled to inspect and copy the will` `who`

§§ `A document that was not properly signed or witnessed`

@export Can the Supreme Court accept a document that was not properly signed or witnessed (or an unwitnessed change to a will) as the deceased's will (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 9(1))? It can if satisfied the deceased intended the document to be their will, looking at how it was made and any evidence of their intentions, including their statements. Documents include electronic ones such as texts or videos.
GIVEN `what` IS A `What was not properly executed` @desc What was not signed and witnessed as the Act requires: nothing (the document was properly executed); the whole document; or an alteration to the will
      `intended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the deceased intended the document to be their will
GIVETH A `Whether the Court may admit the document`
`Can the Court accept the informal document as the will` MEANS
    `s 9(1) -- the Court may admit the informal document as the will` `what` `intended`

@export The deceased tried to revoke their will by a writing that was not signed and witnessed: can the Supreme Court refuse to admit the will to probate (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 9(2))?
GIVEN `executed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the revoking writing was itself signed and witnessed as a will must be
      `intended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the deceased intended the writing to revoke the will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court treat the will as revoked by the informal writing` MEANS
    `s 9(2) -- the Court may refuse probate of a will revoked by an informal writing` `executed` `intended`

@export Can the Registrar of Probates, rather than a judge, decide whether to accept the informal document (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 9(5))? Only if the Court has authorised the Registrar, and either everyone affected consents or the estate is worth no more than the limit the Court has set.
GIVEN `authorised` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Supreme Court has authorised the Registrar to exercise its powers under section 9
      `consent` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether everyone who would be affected by the decision consents
      `estate value` IS A NUMBER @desc The value of the estate, in Australian dollars
      `limit` IS A NUMBER @desc The limit the Supreme Court has set for the Registrar under section 9, in Australian dollars
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Registrar decide the informal will` MEANS
    `s 9(5) -- the Registrar may decide the informal will application` `authorised` `consent` `estate value` `limit`

§§ `Rectifying the will`

@export Can the Supreme Court rectify the deceased's will to carry out their intentions (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 31(1))? Only where it fails to carry them out because of a clerical error or because it does not give effect to their instructions, and the Court is satisfied of that.
GIVEN `reason` IS A `Why the will does not carry out the will-maker's intentions` @desc Why the will does not carry out what the deceased intended: a clerical error was made; it does not give effect to their instructions; or some other reason (a change of mind, a mistake about the law or about what the words mean)
      `satisfied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the will does not carry out the deceased's intentions for that reason
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the will be rectified` MEANS
    `s 31(1) -- the Court may rectify the will` `reason` `satisfied`

@export By what date must I apply to rectify the will (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 31(2))? Within 6 months from the date of the grant of probate; the last day is the same date 6 months later, or the last day of that month if it is shorter.
GIVEN `grant` IS A DATE @desc The date probate (or letters of administration with the will) was granted
GIVETH A DATE
`By when must I apply for rectification` MEANS
    `s 31(2) -- the last day to apply for rectification` `grant`

@export Is my application to rectify the will in time, or can it still be made (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 31(2), (3))? You must apply within 6 months from the grant of probate; the Court may extend that time, even after it ends, but not once the estate has been finally distributed.
GIVEN `granted` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether probate (or letters of administration with the will) has been granted
      `grant` IS A DATE @desc The date of the grant (any date if there is none yet)
      `applied` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether you have already applied to the Court for rectification
      `applied on` IS A DATE @desc The date you applied (any date if you have not)
      `today` IS A DATE @desc Today's date
      `extended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court has extended the time for your application
      `distributed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the final distribution of the estate has been made
GIVETH A `Where my rectification application stands`
`Is my rectification application in time` MEANS
    `s 31(2), (3) -- where my rectification application stands` `granted` `grant` `applied` `applied on` `today` `extended` `distributed`

§§ `A gift to a beneficiary who died before, or soon after, the will-maker`

-- This group arises only where a beneficiary has died: ask "Has a beneficiary died before the will-maker, or soon after?"
-- before showing it.

@export Did the beneficiary survive the will-maker by 30 days (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 39, 45)? A beneficiary who dies within 30 days after the will-maker is treated as if they had died first, unless the will shows a contrary intention.
GIVEN `will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died
      `beneficiary died` IS A DATE @desc The date the beneficiary died
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`Did the beneficiary survive by 30 days` MEANS
    NOT `ss 39, 45 -- the beneficiary did not survive the will-maker by 30 days` `will-maker died` `beneficiary died`

@export What becomes of a gift to a beneficiary who died before the will-maker or within 30 days after (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 35, 39, 45, 46(3))? A child's or grandchild's share can pass to their own children; a failed share of the residue goes to the other shares; any other failed gift falls into the residue; unless the will says otherwise.
GIVEN `alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary is still alive (if so, the dates below are not used)
      `will-maker died` IS A DATE @desc The date the will-maker died
      `beneficiary died` IS A DATE @desc The date the beneficiary died (before or after the will-maker)
      `contrary` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will shows a contrary intention about what happens to the gift (a general condition that beneficiaries must survive the will-maker, or reach an age, does not count)
      `issue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary was the will-maker's child, grandchild or other descendant
      `their issue survived` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether any of the beneficiary's own children or other descendants survived the will-maker by 30 days
      `class or ends` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift is to the will-maker's "issue" as a class without limit, or the beneficiary's interest was to end at or before their death
      `condition unmet` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the beneficiary had not met a condition the will required of them (for example reaching an age)
      `fraction of residue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the gift is a fractional share of the residue, or of the whole estate (for example "one third of the residue")
GIVETH A `What becomes of the gift`
`What becomes of the gift to the beneficiary who died` MEANS
    `ss 35, 39, 45, 46 -- what becomes of the gift` `alive` `will-maker died` `beneficiary died` `contrary` `issue` `their issue survived` `class or ends` `condition unmet` `fraction of residue`

§§ `A will for a relative who lacks capacity`

-- This group arises only where someone wants a will made for a living person who lacks testamentary capacity: ask "Do you
-- want the Court to authorise a will for someone who cannot make one?" before showing it.

@export Can the Supreme Court authorise a will (a statutory will) for someone who lacks testamentary capacity (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) ss 21, 21B)? Anyone may apply. The person must be alive, and the Court must be satisfied they lack capacity, that the proposed will reflects what their intentions would likely be, and that the order is reasonable.
GIVEN `alive` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the person is alive (no order can be made for someone who has died)
      `lacks capacity` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the person does not have testamentary capacity
      `reflects` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied the proposed will reflects what the person's intentions would likely be, or might reasonably be expected to be, if they had capacity
      `reasonable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Court is satisfied it is reasonable in all the circumstances to authorise the will
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Can the Court authorise a will for my relative` MEANS
    `ss 21, 21B -- the Court may authorise a will for the person` `alive` `lacks capacity` `reflects` `reasonable`

@export Am I entitled to appear and be heard on an application for a Court-authorised will (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 21C)?
GIVEN `role` IS A `My role in a statutory will application` @desc Your role: the person the will is for; their lawyer; their attorney under an enduring power of attorney; their guardian or administrator under the Guardianship and Administration Act 2019; someone the Court considers has a genuine interest; or none of these
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Am I entitled to be heard on the statutory will application` MEANS
    `s 21C -- I am entitled to appear and be heard` `role`

@export Is a will made under a Supreme Court order for a person without capacity valid (Wills Act 1997 (Vic) s 25)? It must be in writing, signed by the Registrar and sealed with the Court's seal.
GIVEN `writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the will is in writing
      `registrar` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the Registrar signed it
      `sealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is sealed with the seal of the Court
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`Is the Court-authorised will valid` MEANS
    `s 25 -- the Court-authorised will is valid` `writing` `registrar` `sealed`
